  "sourcesWhyLink": "Why do the two schools differ?",
  "schoolsPara1": "West Bengal has two panjika traditions. Bishuddha Siddhanta (Drik) works from the modern astronomical positions of the Sun and Moon, the same method as the Government of India's national almanac. Surya Siddhanta, followed by Gupta Press and several other almanacs, uses the classical model of the ancient Surya Siddhanta text together with the rules of Raghunandana.",
  "schoolsPara2": "The two models place the Sun and Moon slightly differently, so the same tithi ends at a different time in each, sometimes by a few minutes and sometimes by more. A day takes the tithi that is current at sunrise. So when a tithi ends close to sunrise, the two schools can name different tithis for the same morning, and a festival can fall a day earlier or later.",
  "schoolsPara3": "Neither is a mistake; both are long-standing ways of reckoning. This app shows which tradition every date follows. It offers Bishuddha Siddhanta now, and Surya Siddhanta will come later. If your family follows a particular panjika, compare with it.",
